       rreeqquueesstt__kkeeyy()  attempts to find a key of the given _t_y_p_e with a descrip‐
       tion (name) that matches the specified  _d_e_s_c_r_i_p_t_i_o_n.   If  such  a  key
       could  not be found, then the key is optionally created.  If the key is
       found or created, rreeqquueesstt__kkeeyy() attaches it to the keyring whose ID  is
       specified in _d_e_s_t___k_e_y_r_i_n_g and returns the key’s serial number.

       rreeqquueesstt__kkeeyy()  first  recursively searches for a matching key in all of
       the keyrings  attached  to  the  calling  process.   The  keyrings  are
       searched   in  the  order:  thread‐specific  keyring,  process‐specific
       keyring, and then session keyring.

       If rreeqquueesstt__kkeeyy() is called from a program invoked by  rreeqquueesstt__kkeeyy()  on
       behalf  of  some  other process to generate a key, then the keyrings of
       that other process will be searched next, using  that  other  process’s
       user ID, group ID, supplementary group IDs, and security context to de‐
       termine access.

       The  search  of  the  keyring  tree  is breadth‐first: the keys in each
       keyring searched are checked for a match before any child keyrings  are
       recursed into.  Only keys for which the caller has _s_e_a_r_c_h permission be
       found, and only keyrings for which the caller has _s_e_a_r_c_h permission may
       be searched.

       If  the  key is not found and _c_a_l_l_o_u_t is NULL, then the call fails with
       the error EENNOOKKEEYY.

       If the key is not found and _c_a_l_l_o_u_t is not NULL, then  the  kernel  at‐
       tempts  to invoke a user‐space program to instantiate the key.  The de‐
       tails are given below.

       The _d_e_s_t___k_e_y_r_i_n_g serial number may be that of a valid keyring for which
       the caller has _w_r_i_t_e permission, or it may be one of the following spe‐
       cial keyring IDs:

       KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__TTHHRREEAADD__KKEEYYRRIINNGG
              This  specifies  the  caller’s  thread‐specific   keyring   (see
              tthhrreeaadd--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__PPRROOCCEESSSS__KKEEYYRRIINNGG
              This   specifies  the  caller’s  process‐specific  keyring  (see
              pprroocceessss--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__SSEESSSSIIOONN__KKEEYYRRIINNGG
              This specifies the caller’s session‐specific keyring  (see  sseess‐‐
              ssiioonn--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__UUSSEERR__KKEEYYRRIINNGG
              This   specifies   the   caller’s   UID‐specific   keyring  (see
              uusseerr--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       KKEEYY__SSPPEECC__UUSSEERR__SSEESSSSIIOONN__KKEEYYRRIINNGG
              This specifies the caller’s UID‐session keyring  (see  uusseerr--sseess‐‐
              ssiioonn--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       When  the  _d_e_s_t___k_e_y_r_i_n_g  is  specified as 0 and no key construction has
       been performed, then no additional linking is done.

       Otherwise, if _d_e_s_t___k_e_y_r_i_n_g is 0 and a new key is constructed,  the  new
       key  will be linked to the "default" keyring.  More precisely, when the
       kernel tries to determine to which keyring the  newly  constructed  key
       should  be  linked, it tries the following keyrings, beginning with the
       keyring set via the kkeeyyccttll(2) KKEEYYCCTTLL__SSEETT__RREEQQKKEEYY__KKEEYYRRIINNGG  operation  and
       continuing  in  the  order shown below until it finds the first keyring
       that exists:

       •  The  requestor  keyring  (KKEEYY__RREEQQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__RREEQQUUEESSTTOORR__KKEEYYRRIINNGG,   since
          Linux 2.6.29).

       •  The  thread‐specific  keyring  (KKEEYY__RREEQQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__TTHHRREEAADD__KKEEYYRRIINNGG;  see
          tthhrreeaadd--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       •  The process‐specific keyring  (KKEEYY__RREEQQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__PPRROOCCEESSSS__KKEEYYRRIINNGG;  see
          pprroocceessss--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       •  The  session‐specific  keyring (KKEEYY__RREEQQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__SSEESSSSIIOONN__KKEEYYRRIINNGG; see
          sseessssiioonn--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).

       •  The  session  keyring   for   the   process’s   user   ID   (KKEEYY__RREE‐‐
          QQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__UUSSEERR__SSEESSSSIIOONN__KKEEYYRRIINNGG;  see uusseerr--sseessssiioonn--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).  This
          keyring is expected to always exist.

       •  The   UID‐specific   keyring   (KKEEYY__RREEQQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__UUSSEERR__KKEEYYRRIINNGG;    see
          uusseerr--kkeeyyrriinngg(7)).  This keyring is also expected to always exist.

       If  the kkeeyyccttll(2) KKEEYYCCTTLL__SSEETT__RREEQQKKEEYY__KKEEYYRRIINNGG operation specifies KKEEYY__RREE‐‐
       QQKKEEYY__DDEEFFLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT (or no KKEEYYCCTTLL__SSEETT__RREEQQKKEEYY__KKEEYYRRIINNGG  operation  is  per‐
       formed),  then  the kernel looks for a keyring starting from the begin‐
       ning of the list.

